/*
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 *
 * Provenance: clean-room. Devicetree only; nothing here derives from sdk-ant.
 *
 * THE TIMER AUDIT, AND ITS ANSWER.
 *
 * Three things want a TIMER instance on this part and the plan flagged them as
 * a possible three-way collision:
 *
 *   MPSL              MPSL_TIMER0 is NRF_TIMER10 on nRF54L (mpsl_hwres.h), and
 *                     nrf/subsys/mpsl/init/mpsl_init.c BUILD_ASSERTs
 *                     !DT_NODE_HAS_STATUS_OKAY(timer10) - "MPSL reserves
 *                     TIMER10 on this SoC."
 *   nrf_802154        NRF_802154_TIMER_INSTANCE_NO is 10
 *                     (nrf_802154_peripherals_nrf54l.h), the same instance,
 *                     which the driver takes through MPSL's arbitration rather
 *                     than beside it.
 *   radiant_core      radiant,radio-timer = &timer20, from the repo-root
 *                     boards/ overlay.
 *
 * So the collision is TIMER10 three ways and TIMER20 not at all: MPSL's own
 * BUILD_ASSERT list names timer0, timer10 and timer020 and nothing else, and
 * TIMER20 is not among them on any part. The product overlay does not have to
 * move. That was worth settling from the headers before the DK was free,
 * because if it HAD collided the answer would have been to move the backend's
 * timer to timer21-timer24, which is a change to the shipping board file.
 *
 * timer21 below is this spike's own free-running absolute clock, and it is a
 * FOURTH claim, deliberately made on an instance nobody else lists. Its whole
 * purpose is to answer a question the design rests on: does a TIMER that is not
 * MPSL_TIMER0 keep counting across timeslot boundaries, or does MPSL's start
 * and stop of the timeslot disturb it? radiant_core's t_sync capture and its
 * whole 1 MHz absolute timebase depend on the answer being "it keeps counting",
 * and nothing in MPSL's documentation says so in as many words.
 */

/ {
	chosen {
		spike,free-timer = &timer21;
	};
};

/*
 * Every TIMER on this SoC is `status = "disabled"` by default, and an unclocked
 * peripheral is not addressable - the first register write is a precise bus
 * fault rather than a quiet failure.
 */
&timer21 {
	status = "okay";
};
